WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.390
It's a sad, cinematic truth: many movies are
nowhere near as entertaining as their epic,

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.099
exciting trailers suggest.

00:00:07.099 --> 00:00:11.320
What are trailers but semi-spoilery commercials
designed to get butts in seats anyhow?

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:14.772
Once that thrilling, transcendent trailer
lures you into a sticky theater with a bucket

00:00:14.772 --> 00:00:18.070
of popcorn under your arm, you find out the
horrible truth.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.320
Here are just a few epic trailers that were
wasted on disappointing movies.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:22.800
Prometheus

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.080
After 15 years away from the Alien franchise,
Prometheus promised to be a ground-breaking

00:00:28.089 --> 00:00:30.099
return to form for director Ridley Scott.

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:34.050
Plus, we'd totally get to see, like, billions
of xenomorphs, right?

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.080
Or see where they came from?

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.230
Maybe have coffee with that Space Jockey guy?

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.230
He seemed cool.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.850
The first trailer does an amazing job reminding
the audience of just how awesome the Aliens

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.680
franchise can be, despite its long time out
of theaters and that whole Alien versus Predator

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:49.180
thing.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.040
The name Prometheus fades in just like in
Alien, and we slowly catch glimpses of elements

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.190
from the original movie.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.400
In the space of one minute, it's a masterclass
in visual storytelling, and tons of trailers

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.760
have copied the same format.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.461
But despite average to positive reviews, Prometheus
never hit the same highs as Alien, and it

00:01:05.461 --> 00:01:07.560
just left audiences with even more questions.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.540
Judging by the trailer, however, it could
have been much, much more.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.160
And would it kill Ridley Scott to throw in
a few more xenomorphs?

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.700
The Phantom Menace

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.920
In the dark ages before YouTube, watching
movie trailers was hard.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.770
You had to know which films the trailers were
attached to, and hope your theater was showing

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:24.770
the right ones.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.300
When the trailer for The Phantom Menace dropped,
fans were so hyped that they bought tickets

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.850
to other movies just to see the trailer, not
even staying for the movie afterwards.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.840
All through November 1998, theaters sold out
tickets to movies like A Bug's Life, only

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.420
to have seats empty out before the opening
titles even rolled.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:45.380
We all know how The Phantom Menace turned
out, but the trailer was actually really good.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.590
The opening shot of a fog-covered swamp with
the Force Theme is still spine-tingling.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.450
The visuals look awesome, and even the awkward
Yoda lines feel epic.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.040
It's so good that it almost makes us want
to go back and watch Episode I again.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:57.980
Almost.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.200
In the Heart of the Sea

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.100
Whaling doesn't top many lists of "Things
There Aren't Enough Movies About," but In

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.060
the Heart of the Sea looked like it was ready
to change that.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:13.550
This trailer had everything: beautiful cinematography,
cool special effects shots, some kind of secret

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.830
water monster, and Thor looking badass holding
a spear!

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.620
What's not to love?

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:22.440
The trailer was so good that at least four
million people watched it on purpose, many

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.440
of whom never even knew they wanted to see
a whaling movie.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.280
Unfortunately, the only good things about
the movie were all packed into the trailer.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.920
Despite being stocked with talented actors
and having Ron Howard in the director's chair,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.700
the film never connected with the audience.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:40.000
It earned a meager 42% on Rotten Tomatoes,
with reviewers mostly calling the film boring,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.660
unconvincing, and dry.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.810
Which is saying something, considering the
movie takes place in the water.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.110
In the end, all it did was prove that not
even Chris Hemsworth's rippling muscles can

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.320
save a sinking ship.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.460
Battle: Los Angeles

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.460
Most movie trailers are just made of attractive
scenes slapped together in some kind of coherent

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.459
order, but a few become pieces of art on their
own.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:03.319
Battle: Los Angeles is pure trailer art.

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:07.460
Opening with shots of UFO sightings from around
the world, it gave us a feast of the best

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.750
visuals from the movie, set to Johann Johannsson's
haunting "Part Five."

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.629
The movie looked like a mixture of Saving
Private Ryan and Independence Day; a reinvention

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.220
the alien invasion genre.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.320
Or so we thought.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:23.540
In reality, Battle: Los Angeles earned poor
ratings for a reason.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.390
Roger Ebert gave the film one half of a star
and called it "ugly and stupid", continuing,

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:33.280
"If you attend this crap with friends who
admire it, tactfully inform them they are

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:33.780
idiots."

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.050
The trailer was another story.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:41.239
It was so good, in fact, that two years later,
Edge of Tomorrow copied it shot-for-shot and

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:45.629
even used another Johann Johannsson song as
its soundtrack—only this time, the movie

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:47.740
actually delivered on its trailer's promise.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.440
Tron: Legacy

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:55.100
1982's Tron acquired a cult following after
mediocre returns at the box office.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.930
Decades after the original whiffed, the studio
tried hard to make Tron trendy—and with

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:03.400
their awesome trailer for Tron: Legacy, they
almost succeeded.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.680
There are many things to love about the trailer
for Legacy: nostalgia, great music, neon junk

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.200
everywhere, and who could forget Olivia Wilde?

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.810
11 million views on YouTube proved that the
world was ready for a Tron sequel.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.370
Until it actually came out.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:23.380
USA Today summed up most reviewers' sentiments
by calling it "impenetrable and nonsensical."

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.720
Despite its excellent trailer, it failed to
win over critics or restart the franchise.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.280
Even today, people only really remember it
for its soundtrack, and maybe that's the best

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.060
way to look at it: a two hour long Daft Punk
music video.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.160
The Last Airbender

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.320
It seemed like the movie to that was going
to save M. Night Shyamalan from himself.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:46.110
All he had to do was adapt the story of a
hit franchise, write semi-competent dialogue,

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:50.090
and ride the brand-recognition of The Last
Airbender all the way to the bank.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.870
This was a slam dunk, and the trailer tricked
us into thinking he'd scored.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.700
It had all the elements that we loved from
the cartoon show, faithfully adapted to live

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.200
action.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.920
It even actually looked good.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:01.620
And then it bombed.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.340
At 6% on Rotten Tomatoes, The Last Airbender
is the lowest-rated Shyamalan film, and that

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.420
includes The Happening, the movie where trees
are the villains.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.180
The Atlantic compared the film to

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:17.860
"A chronic illness that worsens and worsens
without ever quite proving terminal."

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:21.330
TIME magazine also summoned up spectres of
death, saying,

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.850
"The actors who didn't get to be in The Last
Airbender are like the passengers who arrived

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:28.229
too late to catch the final flight of the
Hindenburg."

00:05:28.229 --> 00:05:30.630
Oh, the humanity.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.199
But perhaps one fan said it best.

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:35.600
"I want my money back… and I actually wish
I had my hair back too."

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.050
Thanks for watching!

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.610
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.700
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

